# shir_nodes/path_name.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Extract basename or dirname of a path (lowered from `basename`, `dirname`,
# `${p##*/}`, `${p%/*}`).
#
# Backends: Perl File::Basename, JS path.basename/dirname, C strrchr, Rust Path::file_name
# Fallback: sh2.pathName(text, which)
#
# ShIR_TextOps_Strings proves `path_name_sound`, over GNU COMMAND semantics rather
# than bash's string strip (the renderer in all_nodes.rs is documented as "EXACT GNU
# basename/dirname semantics", verified against bash on trailing-slash, all-slash,
# no-slash and multi-slash inputs): strip trailing separators, an all-separator path
# is "/", `basename` is what follows the last separator, `dirname` is the prefix
# before it re-stripped with "" becoming "/" and a slashless path becoming ".".
# Laws: no separator ⟹ basename is the input and dirname is "."; all separators ⟹
# "/" for both; `set (basename p) ⊆ set p`; dirname is never empty. OUT OF MODEL:
# one separator only ("/", 47) — no "\", drive letters or host separator classes;
# no filesystem (no newline stripping, `basename -a`/`-s`); no "."/".." resolution
# or symlink semantics (GNU does not resolve either, but std::path-based renderers
# do, so the node promises no host path library).
# GLOB BOUNDARY, exact: the shells' `${p##*/}` / `${p%/*}` spellings are LONGEST-match
# strips, and ShIR_TextOps_Glob proves that reading over its own matcher: the longest
# strip of `*lit` is the LAST occurrence of the literal
# (TextOps#strip_pre_long_star_lit — `strip_pre_long [PStar, PLit p] t` is drop after
# the last occurrence, identity on a miss), so `${p##*/}` is "the tail after the last
# separator". That is NOT the same function as GNU `basename`:
# TextOps#shell_strip_is_not_gnu_basename exhibits a non-empty path ending in a
# separator, where the glob strip is empty (the star absorbs the trailing separator)
# while `basename` returns the last component, and the two coincide only when there is
# no trailing separator. This node therefore promises the GNU command semantics above,
# proved directly by `path_name_sound`, and a lowering that spells it `${p##*/}` must
# promise the glob law instead; the theory keeps both and proves they are different
# functions, so neither reading is smuggled in as the other. `${p%/*}` is the shortest
# trailing `*lit` strip (strip_suf_short … from the last separator) and `${p%%/*}` the
# longest (TextOps#strip_suf_long_lit_star, from the first).
# Note the generic Ext lowering in
# shir.rs renders split('/').pop() / split('/').slice(0,-1).join('/'), which is
# wrong for "a/b/" and for slashless words — a lowering question, not the spec.

node PathName
tag "PathName"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field which: string
spec: TextOps#path_name_sound
spec: TextOps#strip_pre_long_star_lit
spec: TextOps#shell_strip_is_not_gnu_basename
